  "image_name": "3ca35e9b1814d50e823c144bc7df412f.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes three switchable antibacterial surface designs that use external stimuli to control bacterial attachment, killing, and release. In the top scheme, bacteria interact with a self-assembled monolayer on a gold surface. Changing the applied potential between positive and negative values appears to alter the orientation or exposure of surface groups, switching the surface between a state that allows bacterial binding and one that discourages attachment or promotes detachment.\n\nThe middle scheme shows a wrinkled PDMS surface whose antibacterial behavior depends on mechanical strain. Under plasma treatment and modest prestretch, the surface is prepared in a textured form. Relaxation or limited stretch seems to create topography that traps or holds bacteria, whereas stronger uniaxial stretching smooths or changes the wrinkles so bacteria are released.\n\nThe bottom scheme illustrates a pH-responsive porous PMMA coating loaded with lysozyme. At acidic pH, the antibacterial molecule is retained within grafted polymer chains. At neutral pH, lysozyme is exposed and kills bacteria. At basic pH, the polymer conformation and charge interactions change, leading to release of dead bacteria. Overall, the figure emphasizes dynamic surfaces that can both kill microbes and self-clean afterward.",
